# Signed updates

## Maintenance diagnostics (development, not retrofitted into sealed 0.4.19)

Unexpected API failures display a diagnostic UUID matching the host maintenance
log. Update state writes log that same exception UUID, the attempted job ID,
action and phase, and whether acceptance, a transition or cancellation failed.
Acceptance failures still reject the request before publishing a job receipt or
starting downloads; no filesystem retry, lock deletion or success fallback is
introduced. A later explicit request can retry once the underlying fault is
resolved. Existing fatal journal failures still disable updates and request a
safe host stop.

If acceptance itself cannot save its journal, the API rejects with HTTP503 and
`UPDATE_STATE_UNWRITABLE`, explicitly stating that no new update began. Its
diagnostic UUID matches the filesystem failure log. A separate in-memory
`requestError` keeps the update panel from presenting the preceding successful
check as the result of that rejected request. It does not invent a successful
job receipt or initiate work after a failed save, and is cleared only by a later durably accepted
operation or a new process. An actual OS sharing/permission denial is respected,
not bypassed with lock deletion or implicit repeated writes.

The diagnostic implementation lives under `server/` so an immutable business
release slot includes its own dependency. The stable host passes its diagnostic
function into that slot, retaining a single exception identity across the
bootstrap/update engine and the active business code. Direct server starts use
the versioned implementation locally; the rotating log writer remains in the
stable bootstrap, not a new mandatory root file inside business-only slots.

The native host's anonymous-pipe writer serializes commands in submission order,
not just individual writes under a mutex. A later status request must never
overtake the initial start handshake or an earlier update command when the
ThreadPool schedules tasks in a different order. Status requests remain
coalesced, writes remain asynchronous, and disposal still prevents queued
commands from writing into an exited worker. The strict child protocol is not
relaxed to compensate for reordered commands.

Diagnostics allowlist OS error codes, filesystem operations, numeric errno and
bounded program/Node source locations. Update state and pending-file names are
shown relative to `<update>`; private directory names and other file names are
redacted. Error messages, first stack lines, request URLs/queries, bodies,
cookies, tokens, account IDs and signed envelopes are not copied into these
records. At most three cause records and six source locations per cause are
included. This is not a blanket sanitizer for unrelated existing log producers.
Preserve the diagnostic number and host logs before maintenance. A controlled
Windows no-delete file handle reproduces a rejected rename, but does not prove
the historical EPERM had that same cause.

Do not poll a live Windows journal file to observe an update, including from a
"read-only" diagnostic script. Controlled VM checks observed `EPERM/rename`
while readers held the destination even with `FileShare.ReadWrite|Delete`.
Use the authenticated host update-status API, or a fixture's in-memory status
endpoint. Verify the actual journal only after its owning worker has stopped.
The GUI smoke test uses an in-memory fixture bootstrap snapshot while running,
then its parent verifies the durable phase, selected release and ledger after
normal shutdown. Sharing flags alone are not a noninterference guarantee.

Whole-program native helpers likewise never poll `state.json` while the new
host is committing. Packages declaring `updateCompletionProtocol: 1` publish a
small immutable `done-<job>.json` after the durable terminal state, identity
checks and maintenance release; recovery handoff uses a separate immutable
`handoff-<job>.json`. Existing matching notices are verified, never rewritten,
so an observer cannot block a later journal replacement by reading them. Normal
startup can republish a missing notice for an already committed, identity-matched
job without reapplying the update. Helpers validate company, job, version and
result, and never infer success from an absent notice. Legacy packages without
this capability reopen with an explicit manual-confirmation message instead of
falling back to live-journal polling or claiming automatic completion.

Normal duplicate launch/resume also respects the existing full-update mutex
before inspecting recovery records. The updater's own verified relaunch carries
the already-supported update job flag, including client relaunches, so it does
not deadlock on its own lease.

## Windows 0.4.0 and later

The managed Windows installation now supports signed **whole-program** updates:
business code, the host launcher, the independent Windows client, notification
code, the bundled Node runtime and reviewed database migrations. Configuration,
certificates, ledger, originals, backups, account credentials and the persistent
`电脑端资料` workspace are not update payloads.

The host checks automatically after startup and periodically thereafter. It
downloads and verifies an available release without stopping business work.
The owner confirms installation in System Management. Observed pages must save
and acknowledge the exact maintenance window. Unresponsive or busy pages block
installation; drafts are never cleared or forcibly reloaded.

An application review page is also unfinished work, even though it is not a
dialog. Background synchronization and offline/online transitions retain the
displayed original, checklist and review notes. The displayed decision remains
bound to that application version; a newer server version must be reopened and
checked, not approved with old ticks. An active review or inline original blocks
page refresh, maintenance acknowledgement and native safe exit until the user
finishes or leaves that page. Unsubmitted review notes are page-local, not a
persisted draft: submit or copy them before deliberately leaving the page.

Before replacement, the host verifies a complete backup and tries the declared
migration on a private database copy, checking company identity and financial
records. A separate native helper waits for the old processes to exit, copies
only the signed allowlist with atomic per-file replacement, then verifies the
whole result. The new host must pass identity/version/schema checks before
opening business writes. Journaled interruptions resume through the original
launcher. A same-schema, uncommitted failure can select the prior program.
**After a schema change, incompatible old code is not restarted, and a backup
is never automatically restored over the current ledger.**

Employee installations follow only their paired host's committed release and
still verify it against their own preinstalled publishing key. They download
changed files automatically. An open window displays an update button; a hidden
idle window can apply the host-approved release after its save handshake. No
employee-side ledger is created. Unchanged large files are reused only after
hash verification. Both sides retain the same profile path and company origin.

### Resumable large-file downloads (0.4.10 and later)

Host and employee staging share the same downloader. Signed files of at least
4 MiB use 1 MiB HTTPS byte ranges, at most three requests concurrently. Each
range has a 60-second deadline and at most three attempts, with bounded delays
for transient network failures and HTTP 408/429/500/502/503/504. Invalid ranges,
compression, redirects, unsafe destinations and integrity errors are not retried
as transient failures. Servers must return an exact, uncompressed single 206
range with the declared total and length; unsupported servers fail visibly
rather than silently falling back to another source.

Completed ranges and their checksums are durably saved beneath the external
update storage's `downloads` directory. Cache identity includes the full URL,
signed size, SHA-256 and chunk size. Repeating a download after cancellation,
process exit or a failed attempt rechecks those ranges and requests only missing
ones. A partly received range is downloaded again, not treated as complete.
Cancellation stops and settles all active workers before releasing the update
lease. Corrupt persisted ranges fail explicitly and require maintenance.

The entire assembled file must still match its signed size and SHA-256. Only
then can normal staging seal and reverify the complete release, and installation
still requires the existing signature, authorization, backup and save handshake.
Successful downloads remove their own chunk cache; failed/cancelled downloads
retain it for retry. These records are not business data and never enter the
signed release slot or public website. Abandoned download identities are retained,
not automatically deleted along with unrelated update records.

Small files and manifest requests retain whole-file HTTPS downloads. A large
download without a signed digest and an external resume directory also retains
the original transport; this includes the standalone website verification tool.
Browser downloads of the first installer are a separate path: browser/download
manager resume support is not an installer feature and is not changed here.
Existing 0.4.9 clients must acquire the next signed release with their old
downloader once; only subsequent staging can use the new capability. Do not
modify the sealed 0.4.9 artifacts to bootstrap it.

The current native client correlates each exit/save request with a fresh ID,
negotiated through the page's `hello.exitRequestIds` capability. Late or untagged
replies cannot authorize a different request. After saving, the page rechecks
workspace generation, dirty work, busy operations, files, dialogs and navigation.
The native window disables editing and defers notification navigation until the
attempt ends; a failed attempt restores editing and the deferred target.

For older pages without this capability, the client permits only one unanswered
legacy request. A timeout keeps the window open and blocks another legacy
request until the old reply arrives or a new document loads. That late reply
only clears the wait; a fresh confirmation is still required. New pages also
accept the old native protocol, but older executables must be updated to gain
the native request-correlation protection.

The older business-only format remains supported for compatible releases.
The Linux supervisor supports that format, not the Windows native replacement
protocol. Platform, API, workspace and supervisor compatibility remain explicit.
Windows 0.3.3 and earlier do not gain this native updater by receiving a new
web page: their initial transition requires stopped, backed-up maintenance.
Existing browser drafts must first be synchronized or exported and imported;
they do not automatically become a WebView2 profile.

## Initial trust and publishing identity

`config.example.json` has no feed or publisher key. Release builds include only
`updates\trust-public.json`, containing an Ed25519 **public** key. The private key
belongs on the developer's machine outside the program and delivery trees, in a
directory restricted to the current Windows user and SYSTEM. Keep an independent
encrypted backup. Do not send the key to employees or upload it with the website.

The official policy may also contain `feedUrl`. The first-install wizard validates
this public HTTPS URL and the Ed25519 keys before publication, then creates the
external update configuration automatically. It does not enable company LAN
access or override an existing installation's configuration or source sidecar.
An absent default remains explicitly unconfigured; malformed defaults fail.

Create a publishing identity once on the development computer:

```powershell
.\scripts\New-UpdatePublisher.ps1 `
  -PrivateDirectory 'E:\租赁维护私密\发布身份' `
  -NodePath 'D:\nodejs\node.exe'
```

The intended parent directory must already exist. This command refuses existing
keys or public policy files instead of silently replacing the publishing identity.
It reports paths and the public fingerprint, never the private key contents.

## Preparing a release

After changing and validating the code:

```powershell
.\scripts\Publish-WindowsRelease.ps1 `
  -Version 0.4.1 -Sequence 2 `
  -PrivateKey 'E:\租赁维护私密\发布身份\publisher-private.pem' `
  -OutputDirectory 'E:\租赁发布\0.4.1' `
  -NodePath 'D:\nodejs\node.exe'
```

For the dedicated public GitHub Pages repository, authenticate with the official
GitHub CLI on the maintenance computer and run:

```powershell
node .\scripts\publish-github-pages.cjs `
  'E:\租赁发布\0.4.6\website' 'Sanq1ng/rent-system-updates' `
  'E:\租赁发布\github-pages-0.4.6' --confirm-public
```

The worktree must be new and outside the program and sealed website. The tool
accepts only the signed manifest, verified hash-named blobs, installer and public
download information. It refuses unapproved entries, validates the installer
digest, enforces Git's 100 MiB file limit and Pages' 1 GB site limit, and preserves
previous immutable blobs. Binary Git attributes prevent line-ending and filter
rewrites. Publishing is one commit, so the site cannot promote a manifest before
its files. No ledger, credential or private key is uploaded.

The result means **pushed**, not verified live. Wait for Pages to finish building,
then verify the real HTTPS manifest, signature, installer and every blob download:

```powershell
node .\scripts\publish-github-pages.cjs --verify-live 'E:\租赁发布\0.4.6\website'
```

This uses the product's public-only HTTPS transport and compares every published
file with the sealed site, including the installer and download page. It neither
uploads business information nor changes the release. A different signed release,
damaged download, redirect, compressed response or TLS error fails verification.
Do not assume mainland reachability from one development-network check. A hosting
failure delays updates, not local accounting. The first installer is still not
Authenticode-signed: verify its origin and digest through a trusted channel.

Use a new output directory and a strictly increasing sequence. This prepares
version declarations, rebuilds all three Windows components, packages a data-free
installer, signs a whole-program manifest, and creates a simple download page.
The chosen signing key must match the public policy included in the program.
It does **not** upload anything or provision a hosting account.

Only publish the resulting `website` directory. Upload immutable `blobs` first,
then the installer and page, and switch `manifest.json` last. Keep old blobs
available during downloads. The fixed HTTPS location must serve complete files
directly, without redirects, authentication cookies, query strings or content
compression. SHA-256 checks do not replace HTTPS or the release signature.
The initial installer currently has no Authenticode signature; the Ed25519 update
signature must not be advertised as a Windows publisher certificate.

## Connecting the office host once

After the publishing site is selected, deployed and reachable, stop the office
host and run its bundled maintenance tool locally:

```powershell
.\runtime\node.exe .\maintenance.cjs configure-update-source `
  --config 'C:\公司的软件\配置\config.json' `
  --feed-url 'https://实际发布网站/rent/manifest.json' `
  --source-stopped confirmed
```

This retains the exact previous configuration, uses the preinstalled public key,
checks lifecycle leases and changes only update settings. It does not upload
business data, start a host, create a new ledger, or prove the website is online.
In 0.4.4 and later, these settings are saved beside the selected configuration as
`config.json.updates.json`, not written into the network configuration. The exact
LAN configuration, original snapshot and recovery hashes therefore remain valid.
An existing separate source is backed up before replacement; malformed or linked
records fail explicitly instead of falling back to another feed. Older embedded
`updates` settings remain readable when no separate source exists. Preserve the
separate source with the rest of the configuration when backing up or moving an
installation; signed program updates never replace it. Network disable/restore
does not revert the publishing source.
Restart the original host and verify a real release end to end. Normal transport
requires publicly routed HTTPS; explicit trusted private-network feeds remain
available for controlled testing, not as a shortcut around TLS validation.

Publishing storage is not a remote-control service. Automatic updates do not
allow the developer to browse the office ledger or operate the office computer.
The client can export a deliberately limited diagnostic file; further access
requires a separately authorized support channel.

## Recovery and retention

Keep `更新\releases`, `更新\update-state`, `更新\client-runtime`, and the complete
pre-update backups. Do not delete a journal to "unlock" installation. The native
helper will refuse changed identities, damaged signatures, changed originals,
invalid migration declarations and unsafe paths. Failed copies can be retried;
failed schema compatibility requires maintenance, not an automatic downgrade.

Online server backups do not contain employees' unsynchronized local work.
Keep an independent backup device and a retention policy. The software does not
delete old releases or backups without an explicit maintenance policy.

## Approved office-LAN downloads

The next Windows program supports fetching employee updates from the paired
office host. This is not a new arbitrary private-network feed. The permanent
public feed and publisher keys remain unchanged, and an older client can still
bootstrap through that feed.

Only the current, publisher-verified Windows release can be served. After a fresh
installation, a check of the same published version first compares the complete
local program inventory to its signed manifest. Only an exact match can seed the
cache from local files, without downloading or replacing program blobs; mismatched
same-version content fails visibly. This lets old employees obtain the new host's
approved release even before that host has performed its first online upgrade.
After an upgrade, the normally committed release supplies this cache.

The host exposes a
release-id and SHA-256 addressed file endpoint, never a file-system path, general
proxy, future staged release, business attachment or backup. Single exact byte
ranges are supported. Open handles are checked against the signed file size and
digest; verified identities are cached by inode, size, modification and change
times, so every chunk does not reread an entire large runtime. A changed identity
forces verification again. Readers participate in the ordinary update drain and
are bounded to three simultaneous transfers.

Normal authenticated desktop login obtains a program-only grant, including for
users who do not receive reminders. Grants are bound to the company, installation,
active account, credential identity and existing desktop-device revocation record.
Reminder registration completes first, so issuing a program grant does not
invalidate the reminder token. Grants expire within 30 days; local copies use a
separate Windows-user DPAPI purpose. Explicit logout clears the local grant and
revokes the device through the existing session revocation mechanism. Restore
revokes these device records even with the preceding format-7 restore machinery;
the new restore code also removes the program-grant signing secret.

The employee derives the one allowed private HTTPS origin and CA certificate from
its saved, validated company connection. Credentials travel only in headers to
that origin, never to the public feed or in a URL. Missing authorization or an
ordinary host outage can use the original signed public route with an explicit
notice. Certificate, company-identity, redirect and integrity failures stop the
operation; they are not hidden by another download attempt. If the host is
unreachable before its approved release metadata is obtained, employees do not
independently select a newer public release.

This cache reduces repeated employee Internet downloads. It does not make the
host's initial Internet route faster and is unavailable while the office host is
off or not reachable. Old clients require one normal upgrade before they can use
the LAN path. No firewall changes, shares or extra cloud services are enabled.

## Measuring a small update

Staging reuses valid installed files, completed temporary files and identical new
content already obtained under another signed path. Identical launch aliases do
not need duplicate downloads. The journal records reused file counts, changed-file
body sizes and, when supplied by the transport, actual received program-body bytes
including resumed transfers. HTTP metadata and TLS overhead are not included.

`scripts\Publish-WindowsRelease.ps1` accepts `-PreviousWebsiteDirectory` pointing
to the actual older signed site. It writes `update-download-plan.json` beside the
installer, outside the public site. Both signatures and release ordering are
checked. The estimate assumes valid old files and no previous new-version cache;
it is not a first-install ZIP size. Without a baseline the estimate is explicitly
unknown, not zero.

After building and testing the selected version, `-ReuseVerifiedBuild` keeps the
exact current source-bound native artifacts instead of recompiling them again
while sealing the release. Every existing artifact verifier must pass; failure
stops packaging rather than silently reusing or rebuilding an unexpected program.
Normal version preparation still preserves the existing whole-product native
version/identity contract. This option does not allow stale component versions
to be mixed merely to reduce a download.
